    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A revival of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> that overturned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> rule and became the leading power of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> c. 1250–1310.</li><li>Its ports, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayal">Kayal</a>, grew rich on pearls and on importing thousands of horses each year from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>.</li><li>A succession war and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>’s invasions from 1311 destroyed it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> became a sultanate in 1335.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> wrote that the horses imported into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> country died quickly in the south Indian climate, so the kings had to buy thousands more each year.</p></aside>
    </section>

