    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="Ashoka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka%27s_visit_to_the_Ramagrama_stupa_Sanchi_Stupa_1_Southern_gateway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> were one of the three ancient Tamil royal lineages, named with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangam-literature">Sangam</a> poems and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>’s inscriptions, and associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> on the Vaigai river. After a first period of power in the early medieval centuries, they were conquered by the Chola Empire in the tenth century and spent most of the eleventh and twelfth centuries as subordinate or rebellious princes in the far south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>. Their revival began in the late twelfth century, when the Chola kings were drawn into Pandya succession disputes and Sri Lankan interventions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jatavarman-kulasekara-i">Jatavarman Kulasekara I</a>, who ruled from 1190, asserted independence but was crushed by the Chola king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulottunga-iii">Kulottunga III</a>, who invaded c. 1205 and destroyed the Pandya coronation hall at Madurai. The decisive turn came under his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravarman-sundara-pandya-i">Maravarman Sundara Pandya I</a>, who ruled from 1216. Around 1219 he invaded the Chola country, burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraiyur">Uraiyur</a> and celebrated a victory coronation in the Chola heartland, forcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-chola-iii">Rajaraja III</a> to accept him as overlord; the Chola king was restored only through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> intervention. From this point the Pandyas, rather than the Cholas, were the rising power of the Tamil country, a position that grew in the following decades into supremacy across south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/madurai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madurai.jpg" alt="Madurai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meenakshi_Amman_West_Tower.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KennyOMG, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> country combined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> lands of the Vaigai and Tamraparni valleys, watered by tanks and channels, with a long coast on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mannar">Gulf of Mannar</a> whose pearl fisheries had been famous since antiquity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>, who visited the coast of Ma’bar c. 1292, described the pearl divers, the royal share of their catch and a king who wore a necklace of rubies and pearls. The Pandya ports, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayal">Kayal</a> at the mouth of the Tamraparni, were busy centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a>, exporting pearls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and receiving Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and, above all, horses from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. Both Marco Polo and the Persian historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassaf">Wassaf</a> report that thousands of horses were imported every year because they did not breed well in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and died quickly, and Wassaf describes a merchant family from the Gulf, headed by Jamal al-Din and his brother Taqi al-Din, which controlled much of this trade and held office at the Pandya court. The Yuan court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> exchanged embassies with Ma’bar in the 1280s. Society was organised around temples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> assemblies, agricultural villages and merchant communities, and the kings lavished wealth on the great temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chidambaram">Chidambaram</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Saiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnava</a> devotion was central to public life.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-iii.jpg" alt="Rajendra Chola III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-iii">Rajendra Chola III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rajaraja3_territories2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> armies combined infantry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> and a cavalry that depended on imported horses, and the kings spent heavily to keep it supplied. The empire was created through a sequence of campaigns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravarman-sundara-pandya-i">Maravarman Sundara Pandya I</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> c. 1219. In the 1250s and 1260s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jatavarman-sundara-pandya-i">Jatavarman Sundara Pandya I</a> made the Pandyas paramount in the south: he defeated the Chola king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-iii">Rajendra III</a>, drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> out of the Tamil country and killed the Hoysala king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vira-someshwara">Vira Someshvara</a> c. 1262, overcame the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-cholas">Telugu Chodas of Nellore</a> and fought the Kakatiyas, performing a victory coronation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nellore">Nellore</a>. He also forced the rulers of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna-kingdom">Kingdom of Jaffna</a>, to pay tribute. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravarman-kulasekara-pandya-i">Maravarman Kulasekara Pandya I</a> defeated Rajendra III and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanatha-hoysala">Hoysala Ramanatha</a> c. 1279, after which the Chola dynasty disappears from the record. Around 1284 a Pandya army under the commander Aryacakravarti invaded Sri Lanka, took the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yapahuwa">Yapahuwa</a> and carried off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relic-of-the-tooth-of-the-buddha">Tooth Relic of the Buddha</a>, which King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parakramabahu-iii">Parakramabahu III</a> later recovered by negotiation, according to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/culavamsa">Culavamsa</a></em>. The Pandyas were less able to resist the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, whose mobile cavalry under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> in 1311 while the kingdom was divided by civil war.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marco-polo.jpg" alt="Marco Polo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frontispice_%C3%A9dition_de_Nuremberg_1477_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A distinctive feature of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> government was the sharing of royal authority among several members of the dynasty at the same time. Inscriptions show co-regents and princes ruling jointly or over parts of the kingdom, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>’s report that Ma’bar was ruled by five royal brothers reflects this system, which allowed the dynasty to govern a wide territory but also invited disputes. Kings bore the alternating titles Maravarman and Jatavarman, and their reigns overlapped, which makes the chronology difficult to reconstruct and dependent on careful comparison of inscriptions. The leading rulers were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravarman-sundara-pandya-i">Maravarman Sundara Pandya I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jatavarman-sundara-pandya-i">Jatavarman Sundara Pandya I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravarman-kulasekara-pandya-i">Maravarman Kulasekara Pandya I</a>, the last ruling for some four decades until c. 1310. Below the kings, the administration largely continued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> institutions: the territory was divided into <em>valanadus</em> and <em>nadus</em>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> villages were governed by assemblies called <em>sabhas</em>, other villages by assemblies called <em>urs</em>, and temples managed large endowments of land and gold. Royal orders and grants were recorded in Tamil inscriptions on temple walls. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassaf">Wassaf</a>’s account of Muslim merchants holding office in the ports suggests that the kings also relied on foreign trading communities to manage customs and the horse trade, which was vital for the army.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ranganathaswamy-temple-srirangam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranganathaswamy-temple-srirangam.jpg" alt="Ranganathaswamy Temple, Srirangam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ranganathaswamy-temple-srirangam">Ranganathaswamy Temple, Srirangam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ranganathaswamy_temple_tiruchirappalli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RUPESH MAURYA, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For a few decades in the thirteenth century the Later Pandyan Empire was the dominant power of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with influence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nellore">Nellore</a> in the north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> in the south, and its victories ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a>, which had ruled the Tamil country for more than three centuries. Its ports linked south India with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, the Arab world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan China</a>, and foreign visitors recorded the wealth of its pearl and horse trades: the accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassaf">Wassaf</a> are among the most detailed medieval descriptions of a south Indian kingdom. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> were great patrons of temples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jatavarman-sundara-pandya-i">Jatavarman Sundara Pandya I</a> covered the shrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranganathaswamy-temple-srirangam">Ranganatha temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a> with gilded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and endowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chidambaram">Chidambaram</a>, earning the title ‘the king who covered the temple with gold’, and the dynasty contributed to the early phase of the tall gateway towers, the <em>gopurams</em>, that came to dominate Tamil temple architecture. Its intervention in Sri Lanka contributed to the shift of Sinhalese political centres away from the north and to the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna-kingdom">Tamil Kingdom of Jaffna</a> under the Aryacakravarti rulers, who were connected with the Pandya expeditions. After the empire fell, the Pandya name survived through the Tenkasi Pandyas, who ruled in the far south into the sixteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wassaf"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wassaf.jpg" alt="Wassaf" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wassaf">Wassaf</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Modern_Illustration_in_Baysunghur_Tarikh-i_Wassaf2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Baysunghur's court &amp;amp; library., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire’s collapse began with a succession dispute. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassaf">Wassaf</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravarman-kulasekara-pandya-i">Maravarman Kulasekara Pandya I</a> favoured his son Vira <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a>, born of a concubine, over the legitimate heir Sundara Pandya, who killed his father c. 1310. The ensuing civil war between the two brothers coincided with the southern campaigns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. In 1311 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a>, the general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>, marched south from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> capital, plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> and other towns and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> with enormous treasure, although he did not establish lasting control. The weakened Pandyas were also attacked from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, where the Venad ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi-varma-kulasekhara">Ravivarman Kulasekhara</a> advanced as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>. In 1323 an army of the Tughluq dynasty under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-khan">Ulugh Khan</a>, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a>, conquered Madurai and made the Pandya lands the province of Ma’bar. In 1335 its governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-ahsan-shah">Jalal al-Din Ahsan Shah</a>, rebelled and founded the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-sultanate">Madurai Sultanate</a>. Pandya princes continued to hold territory in the far south, around Tirunelveli, but by the 1340s they no longer formed a significant power. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> destroyed the Madurai Sultanate in 1378, the Pandyas survived as Vijayanagara subordinates, later known as the Tenkasi Pandyas.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Later Pandyan Empire revived an ancient Tamil dynasty, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> and briefly dominated south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> in the thirteenth century, growing rich on pearls and the horse trade before succession war and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>’s invasions destroyed it.</em></p>

